612	446	567	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	SQL Server checkpoints flush dirty pages to disk. On the secondary, both redo and checkpoint generate disk I/O. With automatic checkpointing (target_recovery_time = 0), checkpoints are triggered by recovery interval (default 0 = ~1 minute), which can cause I/O spikes. Indirect checkpoints (target_recovery_time > 0, default 60s in SQL 2016+) spread I/O more evenly. If the secondary has slow storage, checkpoint I/O competes with redo I/O. Mitigation: use indirect checkpoints with a reasonable target (60-120 seconds), separate data and log files on different volumes, ensure adequate I/O bandwidth.	2026-02-23 20:18:31.870167
