1172	710	1121	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	When the active working set (frequently accessed data pages) exceeds the buffer pool, SQL Server enters a constant eviction cycle: pages are read from disk, used briefly, then evicted to make room for other pages, only to be re-read again. This is visible as low PLE, high Page reads/sec, and high Lazy writes/sec. sys.dm_os_buffer_descriptors shows exactly which databases and how many pages are cached. Fix options: (1) increase max server memory if hardware permits, (2) add physical RAM to the server, (3) optimize queries to scan fewer pages (add covering indexes, reduce table scans), (4) implement table partitioning to reduce the hot data set, (5) archive cold data to reduce database size.	2026-02-23 20:18:31.870527
