1409	815	1357	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	SQL Server defaults to 'user connections' = 0, which means dynamically allocated up to 32,767. This is the Microsoft-recommended default, but without additional controls it means the server has no defense against connection overload. Each connection consumes memory (~1.5 MB for session state, network buffers, and sort/hash memory grants), a worker thread when active, and scheduler time. Resource Governor (Enterprise Edition only) can limit connections per workload group, but many deployments do not configure it. Fix: set a reasonable 'user connections' limit based on expected workload (sp_configure 'user connections', N), configure Resource Governor for workload isolation, implement logon triggers to enforce per-application connection limits, and set up monitoring alerts when connection count exceeds 70% of capacity.	2026-02-23 20:18:31.870675
