1578	889	1526	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	SQL Server implements group commit automatically: when multiple transactions commit concurrently, their log records are flushed together in a single I/O. Effective group commit produces larger, fewer log writes (higher bytes/write). When avg_write_bytes is very small (< 4KB), it means each commit is flushing individually — typically caused by: (1) low concurrency (only one transaction committing at a time), (2) very fast storage that completes writes before additional commits accumulate, (3) delayed durability disabled. Fix: (1) increase transaction concurrency, (2) enable delayed durability to explicitly batch flushes, (3) accept the overhead if concurrency is inherently low.	2026-02-23 20:18:31.87078
