2642	1373	2579	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	In SQL Server, triggers execute within the same transaction as the DML statement. If a trigger raises an error (severity >= 11) or executes ROLLBACK, the entire transaction is aborted. Common trigger failure causes: (1) trigger enforces business rules and rejects the change, (2) trigger inserts into audit table that has constraint violations, (3) trigger references linked servers that are unavailable, (4) trigger logic has bugs (divide by zero, NULL reference). Fix: (1) move validation logic to the application or stored procedure BEFORE the DML, (2) use TRY/CATCH inside triggers with appropriate error handling, (3) replace AFTER triggers with application-side logic where possible, (4) test triggers with edge cases (multi-row DML, NULL values, concurrent access).	2026-02-23 20:18:31.871461
