2904	1510	2840	1	confirmed	\N	ruled_out	\N	In development, it is common to give all accounts sysadmin or CONTROL SERVER because permission errors slow down development. When these practices carry over to production: (1) application accounts with sysadmin can access every database, (2) a SQL injection vulnerability becomes a full server compromise, (3) any application bug can corrupt any database. Detection: any active session from an application (not SSMS, not SQL Agent) running under sysadmin is a strong indicator. Fix: (1) create per-application logins with minimum permissions, (2) use contained database users where possible, (3) implement separate dev/staging/prod credential management.	2026-02-23 20:18:31.871633
2905	1511	2841	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Operational convenience is a major driver of privilege creep: DBAs use their sysadmin account for everything (including running application queries, troubleshooting, data fixes) because switching contexts is inconvenient. The risk: (1) accidental DROP TABLE on wrong database, (2) no audit trail distinguishing admin vs routine work, (3) if the sysadmin credential is compromised, full server compromise. Fix: (1) DBAs should have separate admin and routine accounts, (2) use EXECUTE AS for context-switching during troubleshooting, (3) implement Privileged Access Management (PAM) with just-in-time elevation.	2026-02-23 20:18:31.871634
