2917	1517	2853	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Legacy applications (especially those from the SQL Server 2000/2005 era) were often built with the assumption that the application login IS the dbo. Hardcoded patterns: (1) unqualified object references (SELECT * FROM Orders instead of app.Orders), (2) CREATE TABLE without schema specification (defaults to dbo), (3) dynamic SQL that builds statements assuming dbo context. Restricting these applications requires either: (1) modifying all SQL statements to use explicit schema qualification, (2) creating a custom schema and using DEFAULT_SCHEMA, (3) accepting the risk and documenting the exception. Fix: for new development, always use explicit schema qualification and custom schemas; for legacy applications, create a containment plan with compensating controls (audit, network isolation).	2026-02-23 20:18:31.871641
