2923	1520	2859	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Centralized identity governance requires: (1) Windows Authentication or Azure AD authentication as primary auth method, (2) AD group-based access (add user to AD group = grant SQL access), (3) AD group removal = automatic access revocation (no SQL Server action needed), (4) centralized password policy via Group Policy. Without this: (1) each SQL login is a separately managed credential, (2) offboarding requires finding and disabling logins on every SQL Server instance, (3) password policies are inconsistent per instance, (4) there is no single source of truth for who has access to what. Fix: (1) migrate SQL logins to Windows/Azure AD authentication, (2) create AD groups mapped to database roles (e.g., AD group 'SQL_AppName_Readers' mapped to db_datareader), (3) implement Azure AD Conditional Access for cloud instances, (4) use Azure Purview or similar IAM tools for access governance.	2026-02-23 20:18:31.871646
