3027	1574	2963	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Cross-database ownership chaining only bypasses permission checks when the calling object's owner matches the referenced object's owner across the database boundary. When owners differ, the chain breaks and SQL Server evaluates permissions normally. This creates a confusing pattern: sometimes cross-database access works (same owner), sometimes it fails (different owner), with no obvious error message explaining why. Administrators who do not understand this may: (1) set all databases to the same owner (creating excessive trust), (2) grant broad permissions to compensate (defeating the purpose), or (3) enable TRUSTWORTHY (escalating risk). Fix: disable chaining, use explicit permissions, document the ownership chaining mechanic for the team.	2026-02-23 20:18:31.871779
